129
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:27,000
So let's get started, shall we?

130
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:32,000
Andrew Skurka has won a lot of attention for his solo long distance backpacking trips.

131
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:38,000
For instance, he hiked a 4,700 mile six month expedition in Alaska and the Yukon,

132
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:41,000
which would be like a lifetime achievement for most of us,

133
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
But that was basically one of the shorter ones.

134
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:53,000
He covered 6,800 and 75 miles in seven months following the Great Western Loop in the United States,

135
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
But that's still not the longest one.

136
00:07:55,000 --> 00:08:04,000
He walked 7,775 miles- just saying that makes my feet hurt- in an 11 month sea to sea route across the US,

137
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:10,000
And he figures that in the last decade or so he's covered 30,000 plus miles

138
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:15,000
And that's the equivalent of traveling 1.2 times around the Earth's equator.

139
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,000
He's been named adventurer of the year by both outside and the National Geographic Adventure

140
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:24,000
Magazine, as well as Person of the Year by Backpacker Magazine.

141
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:29,000
He's also the author of the recently published Ultimate Hiker's Gear Guide from National Geographic,

142
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:35,000
And if you look at your March- April edition of Scouting Magazine, there he is right there on the cover.

143
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:38,000
I'm happy to welcome Andrew to the Scout Master Podcast.

144
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:40,000
Good morning, Andrew. How are you?

145
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:42,000
Good morning, Thanks very much for having me.

146
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:45,000
Well, no, I really appreciate you spending the time.

147
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:48,000
How in the world. did you end up doing this?

148
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
I think I sort of stumbled into it.

149
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:57,000
I never set out with the intention of making this into a lifestyle,

150
00:08:57,000 --> 00:09:01,000
But I did my first long distance trip when I was still in college.

151
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:04,000
It was 2002, when I did the Appalachian Trail

152
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:07,000
And after graduating I did that sea to sea trip

153
00:09:07,000 --> 00:09:09,000
And I think things just sort of escalated from there

154
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:15,000
And I was fortunate that I hadn't developed any extensive habits

155
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:18,000
or taken on any extensive responsibilities

156
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:22,000
And that kind of allowed me to be like a dirtbag hiker, if you will,

157
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Kind of like a dirtbag climber, but I was hiking instead

158
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:33,000
And slowly over time I was able to build it into something that was economically sustainable as well.

159
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
We're talking with volunteer scout leaders and we're all parents.

160
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,000
So you are getting ready to graduate from college.

161
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:42,000
You were at Duke, right,

162
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:43,000
Correct?

163
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:48,000
And you decide that you're going to go and do a through hike of the Appalachian Trail,

164
00:09:48,000 --> 00:09:50,000
If I have the timeline right there,

165
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
Yeah, I did it before I graduated.

166
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:54,000
It was actually between semester-

167
00:09:54,000 --> 00:09:57,000
My son's name is Andrew, by the way-

168
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
And he came to us like in the fall semester of his junior.

169
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,000
He says: you know what

170
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,000
I think I'd like to go and spend three or four months on the Appalachian Trail.

171
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:11,000
How did your parents react to that when you told them that you had this great idea?

172
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
The Appalachian Trail is part of our larger plan,

173
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:17,000
So the economy at the time was pretty bad.

174
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:20,000
It was just difficult to find anything worth doing for the summer.

175
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:29,000
So I kind of pitched it as something I could do this summer, that I could sort of knock off my bucket list

176
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:36,000
And that way, when the economy is a little better and my odds of finding something worth doing were better,

177
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,000
I would be available for it.

178
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:40,000
And they were thrilled about this.

179
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:44,000
They weren't thrilled, but they were okay with it.

180
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:48,000
The plan made sense then.

181
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:54,000
They just didn't expect that when I did the Appalachian Trail, that wasn't going to be enough.

182
00:10:54,000 --> 00:11:01,000
So the trip that they really got upset about was that sea-to-sea trip,

183
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:10,000
because instead of doing something with my $150,000 education,

184
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:26,000
I was instead, in their view, sort of pissing it away by taking on this useless, dangerous,

185
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,000
Useless, dangerous, crazy.

186
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:31,000
Yeah.

187
00:11:31,000 --> 00:11:38,000
But I think my feeling on it was that I was at the one point in my life where I really could do something like this.

188
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
I was young, I didn't have any responsibilities,

189
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:46,000
I hadn't committed to a career, to a woman, to a home. I hadn't done any of that stuff,

190
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
So I was going to do anything like this and that was the time

191
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:57,000
And I stuck with that decision because I had made the realization that these lifestyle decisions are mind-making

192
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:05,000
And there's a lot of pressure from society, from parents, from peers, to take that very conventional track

193
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:14,000
which is your college degree, job, house or wife, house, kids, retired at 65.

194
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,000
And there's a lot of pressure to do that

195
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:21,000
And I had made this realization that if I wanted to make a different decision I could do that

196
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:29,000
And you know. so the message I see in that for us folks is: listen to your kids carefully,

197
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Listen to what their plans are, what they're thinking about and, hey, you know, down the road, look what can happen.

198
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:40,000
The traditional way out is not the only way out,

199
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:43,000
And Andrew's a real good example of that.

200
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:53,000
I'd also add, if I could, I think I'd also add that I think any experience in which your child is putting themselves out of their comfort zones

201
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:00,000
and is having an opportunity to grow will probably be a good thing in one way or another.

202
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:02,000
Absolutely, I absolutely agree with you

203
00:13:02,000 --> 00:13:08,000
And I think scouting- we kind of major in that, putting people outside their comfort zone in a lot of ways.

204
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:15,000
So you talk about the first real backpacking trip. you went on as being that you left.

205
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:19,000
you set out from Springer Mountain in Georgia on the Appalachian Trail.

206
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:20,000
How did that go?

207
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:22,000
Oh, it was bad.

208
00:13:22,000 --> 00:13:24,000
It was bad.

209
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:26,000
So it was my first Congressman's trip.

210
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:28,000
It was my first backpacking trip,

211
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:36,000
And now I describe myself as a camper by default,

212
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:42,000
which is a backpacker that takes too much, because they're basically packing their fears.

213
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
They justify a lot of your decisions on the grounds of what if or just in case,

214
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,000
And they just don't know.

215
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:54,000
I also shared a lot of conventional gear, which is because I just didn't know any better.

216
00:13:54,000 --> 00:14:00,000
I thought that what the industry was telling me and what an REI catalog was telling me

217
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,000
that that was the right thing,

218
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:12,000
And then finally, I had. no, I wasn't able to use any gear that required any special thought or skill to use.

219
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
Everything had to be foolproof.

220
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:18,000
So I wasn't capable of setting up the tarp, so I had to use a double wall pen.

221
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:26,000
I didn't know how to. I didn't know how to select a good campsite.

222
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
I didn't know how to pack a bag.

223
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
I didn't know how to pack a bag correctly.

224
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:37,000
So it was as necessary that I had some big bomb-proof suspension pack where it didn't really matter

225
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:38,000
how I loaded it,

226
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:40,000
It was in there and it all sort of fit at the same.

227
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
So I didn't have any skills.

228
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:45,000
So that was kind of my three main problems.

229
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:50,000
Unfortunately, a lot of beginner and, to that matter, I think, still a lot of intermediate backpackers

230
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:58,000
are like that and their experience is just so miserable, their hiking experience is so miserable

231
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:02,000
that they end up just wanting to camp because they just they can't enjoy hiking.

232
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:08,000
I think that your experience that you had is played out in a slightly smaller scale

233
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:12,000
by scouts, like hundreds and hundreds of times every year

234
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
They give this a shot

235
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:16,000
Tens of thousands of times,

236
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Tens of thousands of times. yeah,

237
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:21,000
Probably you know there are 20,000 kids who go through film on every summer

